  "reason": "1) Evidence/uncertainty: No experiments have been completed, so we have zero information about which modalities, encoders, profile settings, or fusion strategies are effective. The most fundamental uncertainty is whether the RNA signal alone (with the simplest possible encoder) carries useful discriminative information at all. 2) What this tests: config_001 (RNA-only, single profile, PCA n2000) is the absolute minimal baseline—no learned encoder, no multi-profile aggregation, no image modality, no fusion. It establishes the performance floor for the RNA modality and confirms the pipeline works end-to-end. 3) Why it is the best use of the first experiment: It is the fastest to run (PCA requires no training), it isolates a single modality with the simplest representation, and it provides a concrete reference number against which every subsequent design choice (better RNA encoders, multi-profile aggregation, image modality, multimodal fusion) can be measured. Without this floor, we cannot tell whether added complexity is actually helping or merely adding noise. Starting here respects the principle of not assuming complex models outperform simple ones and reduces the greatest initial uncertainty with the least risk of confounding."
